# Research at PEARL

We study how robots can **infer**, **anticipate**, and **adapt** to human goals during real-world collaboration—not just execute fixed scripts.

Our work sits at the intersection of human-robot interaction, robot learning, and socially assistive systems. We build algorithms and deployable platforms, then validate them with human participants in lab studies and in-the-wild deployments.

## Goal inference and adaptation

How can a robot figure out what a person wants when goals are ambiguous, unspoken, or change mid-task? We develop methods that combine observed actions, language, and planning—so robots can assist without becoming a nuisance.

Representative directions:

- Open-ended goal inference from actions and language (BALI)
- Detecting and adapting when people change their minds during collaboration
- Critical decision points for faster strategy and goal inference

## Human-robot collaboration

We design interaction mechanisms that let people shape robot behavior through natural communication—verbal commands, demonstrations, and transparent policy overlays—while robots maintain fluent task performance.

## Socially assistive robotics

We build and deploy systems that support wellbeing in everyday environments, including mental health applications and robot-led activities for people living with dementia and their care partners.

## Perception and learning

We study how robots should represent the visual world when humans are the ultimate judge of success—from recycling robots to satellite image segmentation—with active learning and human-tailored representations.

